<GuideLayout title="Intermediate 1.4 · Farming, Desert Treasure & Earth Orbs" current="intermediate">
  <div class="page-grid">
    <aside class="sidebar"><IntermediateNav current="1-4" /></aside>
    <article class="content">
      <header class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-kicker">Intermediate · Chapter 1.4</div>
        <h1 class="chapter-title">Convert the early foundation into a real midgame account.</h1>
        <p class="chapter-summary">This subsection has four connected payoffs: protection prayers, a passive Farming foundation that leads into contracts when eligible, Ancient Magicks, and a large Earth-orb stock for the Chapter 2 battlestaff cash loop. The quest order below is deliberately linear, with exact future items called out only when they save a meaningful return trip.</p>
      </header>

      <ChapterGoals
        summary="You are finishing the quest/stat web prepared in 1.3 and deliberately preparing the first several Chapter 2 blocks. The major breakpoints are 43 Prayer, 18 Slayer into Animal Magnetism, 45 Ranged, a passive Farming routine, 46 Hunter, Desert Treasure, 108 Paddewwa tablets and 1,590 charged Earth orbs. Claim the Chapter 1 Crafting quest rewards before choosing a mushroom-pie boost or a measured permanent-level bridge. Also bank the small carryovers Chapter 2 expects: a spare bucket of sap, a harralander, 2,000 stardust and—if birdhouse RNG cooperates—two growing teak trees."
        stats={["70 Woodcutting — 75 only if you still lack 16 magic logs", "43 Prayer · 45 Ranged · 20+ Slayer · 46 Hunter", "40 Herblore · 42 Smithing · live Crafting check for the battlestaff start", "Farming routine started; live level, not a fixed Chapter 1 exit", "63 Magic · live 53 Thieving check for Desert Treasure", "39 Construction target for the Chapter 2 handoff", "Optional AFK detour: 65 Attack / 65 Strength / 65 Defence"]}
        unlocks={["Earth-battlestaff start: actual 58 Crafting or enough mushroom-pie boosts", "The Grand Tree + Eyes of Glouphrie", "Garden of Tranquillity + Eadgar’s Ruse", "Passive Farming; easy contracts when eligible", "My Arm’s Big Adventure", "Ava’s attractor via Animal Magnetism", "The Hand in the Sand / Bert's daily sand", "Ancient Magicks via Desert Treasure", "108 Paddewwa tablets", "1,590 charged Earth orbs"]}
      />

      <div class="callout"><strong>Full Chapter 1 finish line:</strong> roughly 46 Attack · 50 Strength · 34 Defence · 39 Hitpoints · 45 Ranged · 43 Prayer · 63 Magic · 24 Runecraft · 39 Construction · actual 50+ Agility · 40 Herblore · <strong>live Thieving (at least 53 for Desert Treasure), full Rogue and the 1.2 liquid-cash stop</strong> · <strong>live Crafting level sufficient to start Earth battlestaves with or without a pie</strong> · roughly 47–48 Fletching (46 is the deliberate checkpoint) · 20+ Slayer · 46 Hunter · 44 Mining · 42 Smithing · actual 65+ Fishing · 60 Cooking · 89 Firemaking · 70 Woodcutting · <strong>Farming routine started, with its exit level determined by your real runs and seeds</strong>. Fishing may be higher if ordinary Barbarian Fishing supplied the relaxed Agility remainder; neither 74 Fishing nor 77 Thieving is a required checkpoint. Farming 49 is checked at Fairytale II in 2.6 and actual 70 before Song of the Elves.</div>

<TrainingOptions id="i14-woodcutting-training" informational={true} skill="Woodcutting" target="actual 70; caps are optional" why="Actual 70 Woodcutting is a later Song of the Elves gate. Sulliusceps overlap with this grind and may supply mushroom-pie caps, but Chapter 1 has not yet claimed Animal Magnetism, Enlightened Journey or The Hand in the Sand Crafting XP. Do not decide to grind permanent 58 Crafting—or stay solely for caps—before those rewards land." recommended="Train normally to 65, then use Sulliusceps for some or all of 65→70 if you enjoy the loop. Bank up to five caps if they arrive reasonably, but stop at actual 70 Woodcutting even if they do not. Decide on pies only at the final live Crafting check." relaxed="Forestry, ordinary teaks or another comfortable method to actual 70 are fully valid. No Sulliuscep cap is a required outcome; a later live Crafting bridge can replace the boost." resource="Chapter 1 also needs 16 magic logs; Wintertodt may already have supplied them. If you reach 70 and still have fewer than 16 banked, continue to 75 and cut the missing logs yourself. If you did not have 60 Woodcutting during the Zeah block in 1.3, buy the rune and mithril axes at the Woodcutting Guild when you reach it." fast="2-tick teaks are optional and never assumed." />

      <RouteStep id="i14-sulliuscep-caps" number="1" title="Reach actual 70 Woodcutting; bank Sulliuscep caps only if convenient">
        <p><strong>If you have never done Fossil Island or Sulliusceps before, do not treat this as “just go chop mushrooms.”</strong> Sulliusceps are giant mushrooms inside the <strong>Tar Swamp on Fossil Island</strong>. Only one of six locations is active for your character at a time; after you chop the active mushroom down, the next one becomes active and you continue around the route. The first trip is the confusing one. Once the southern teleport is unlocked and you have seen the loop once, repeated clears are much simpler.</p>
        <p><strong>Bring:</strong> your <strong>rune axe</strong> (or dragon axe if Wintertodt gave you one), a <strong>rake</strong>, <strong>antipoison</strong>, plenty of <strong>food</strong>, Fossil Island access such as a <strong>digsite pendant</strong>, and a teleport out. Keep several free inventory spaces for caps, fossils and other drops. The swamp has dangerous tar monsters and poisonous spine mushrooms; chopping is fairly low-attention, but moving between spots is not walk-away AFK.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Get into the swamp:</strong> use the Fossil Island mushtree network to reach <strong>Mushroom Meadow</strong>, then head south into the Tar Swamp. Cut blocking vines/roots with your axe and <strong>rake the ground vines</strong> when the route requires it.</li>
          <li><strong>Unlock Sticky Swamp immediately:</strong> on the first lap, activate the <strong>Sticky Swamp Magic Mushtree</strong> near the southern/bottom part of the route. From then on you can teleport straight back to the useful end of the swamp instead of repeating the ugly entrance every trip.</li>
          <li><strong>Avoid the hazards instead of fighting them:</strong> do not intentionally fight tar monsters. Keep moving, eat when needed, and use terrain to get away. Antipoison is for the spine-mushroom poison.</li>
          <li><strong>Follow the rotation:</strong> find the active Sulliuscep, chop it until it depletes, then move to the next active location. After your first complete lap, this becomes a repeatable circuit rather than a scavenger hunt.</li>
          <li><strong>Bank caps if they arrive while training to 70.</strong> Five caps can make four mushroom pies later, but they are not a condition for finishing this step. Birdhouse runs do not award Sulliuscep caps.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Stop at actual 70 Woodcutting.</strong> A five-cap hunt may take roughly 45–75 minutes once familiar, but its random tail is not part of required progress. If five caps arrived, bank them. If not, continue the quests; their Crafting rewards may reduce or eliminate any later bridge. The required checkbox here is Woodcutting 70 only.</p>
        <p><a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Sulliuscep/Strategies" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the OSRS Wiki Sulliuscep strategy</a> before the first run and keep its swamp-route image handy. Keep useful unidentified fossils you receive; they can be identified for Varrock Museum displays/Kudos later.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <div class="later-note"><strong>Hold the Crafting decision:</strong> keep any caps banked. Complete the coming quests and their Crafting rewards first; the final Chapter 1 checkpoint explains the pie recipe or a live-XP permanent bridge only if needed.</div>

      <RouteStep id="i14-gnomes" number="2" title="Complete The Grand Tree, then The Eyes of Glouphrie">
        <p>Complete <strong>The Grand Tree</strong> from start to finish. Then complete <strong>The Eyes of Glouphrie</strong>. These are now one clean Gnome Stronghold quest block rather than quests you started hours ago and periodically revisited.</p>
        <p>After Eyes of Glouphrie, enchant the <strong>crystal saw seed</strong>. Keep the crystal saw; it becomes a useful Construction booster later.</p>
        <p>Before leaving this block, bring a <strong>knife + 1 empty bucket</strong> to an <strong>evergreen tree</strong> (the trees between Barbarian Village and Falador are convenient). Use the knife on the tree with the bucket in your inventory and bank <strong>1 bucket of sap</strong>. Early Chapter 2 needs it for <strong>Icthlarin's Little Helper</strong>; collecting it now prevents an otherwise pointless return trip.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-garden" number="3" title="Complete Garden of Tranquillity and bank the compost with a purpose">
        <p>You already have at least <strong>33 Farming</strong> from the Chapter 1.3 quest rewards and Creature of Fenkenstrain is complete, so do <strong>Garden of Tranquillity</strong> from start to finish now. Bring the <strong>ring of charos</strong> from 1.3.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Farming math:</strong> By this point the route has awarded at least <strong>19,500 Farming XP</strong>: 1,000 from the Goblin generals subquest, 3,500 from Fairytale I, 5,000 from Forgettable Tale, and 10,000 from The Garden of Death. That is at least <strong>level 33</strong>. Garden of Tranquillity adds another <strong>5,000 XP</strong>, so both <strong>Enlightened Journey (30 Farming)</strong> and <strong>My Arm's Big Adventure (29 Farming)</strong> are already covered with no hidden farm run.</div>
        <p>During the Farming work, bank <strong>15 supercompost</strong>. Label the reason mentally:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>7 supercompost are reserved for My Arm’s Big Adventure.</strong></li>
          <li>The remaining <strong>8</strong> are a small Farming/Herblore buffer and can be converted/used normally after My Arm is complete.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If you use compost potions for early Herblore, one dose can upgrade a normal compost bin/bucket to supercompost; the volcanic ash from Fossil Island can later turn supercompost into ultracompost.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Start the Chapter 2 teak timer now:</strong> Garden of Tranquillity pushes the guaranteed quest XP past <strong>35 Farming</strong>. If you have the <strong>2 teak seeds</strong> reserved from birdhouses, put each seed in a <strong>filled plant pot</strong> with a gardening trowel, water it, wait for the sapling, then plant the two saplings in the <strong>two western Fossil Island hardwood patches</strong> with a spade. Use <strong>supercompost/ultracompost</strong> on the patches. Teaks take roughly three days to grow, and early Chapter 2 can use two mature player-grown trees for the plank grind. If birdhouse RNG has given you only one or zero seeds, plant what you have and continue birdhouse/eligible Farming Contract runs; <strong>Chapter 2.1 has a Castle Wars teak fallback</strong>, so neither chapter waits on the missing seed or growth timer.</div>
        <p><strong>Start ordinary tree and fruit-tree saplings now too, if seeds are banked.</strong> Open the <a href="#i14-farming-training">first-timer tree circuit below</a> now if you need patch directions, then return to quest 4 and check mature trees at later logins. This is not a 62-Farming stop sign. Keep any western Fossil Island timber teaks standing after their first health check until Chapter 2.1 has its logs.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-eadgar" number="4" title="Complete Eadgar’s Ruse and take the two future goutweed now">
        <p>You reached <strong>31 Herblore</strong> in 1.3, so complete <strong>Eadgar’s Ruse</strong> in one pass with no boost required. The quest awards <strong>11,000 Herblore XP</strong>.</p>
        <p>When you reach the goutweed storeroom, collect <strong>3 goutweed total</strong> before leaving. One is used to finish Eadgar’s Ruse; bank the <strong>2 extras</strong> for <strong>Dream Mentor</strong> and <strong>Dragon Slayer II</strong>. This is a worthwhile future pickup because returning to the storeroom is genuinely annoying.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-kourend-easy" number="5" title="Finish Kourend & Kebos Easy and put the 2,500 XP lamp on Herblore">
        <p><strong>Do not grind Herblore to 40.</strong> BruhSailer reaches 40 through guaranteed quest/diary XP plus incidental potion XP. Because this guide only guarantees the exact <strong>31 Herblore</strong> needed for Eadgar’s Ruse, we make the diary lamp explicit so the breakpoint is deterministic.</p>
        <div class="callout"><strong>Guaranteed Herblore math from the minimum route:</strong> the actual-31 checkpoint in 1.3 already includes Jungle Potion's 775 XP. From the level-31 floor of 14,833 XP, add <strong>Eadgar’s Ruse</strong> 11,000, this diary lamp 2,500, and <strong>My Arm’s Big Adventure</strong> 10,000: at least <strong>38,333 Herblore XP</strong>, above the <strong>37,224 XP</strong> needed for level 40. Do not count Jungle Potion twice. Any Wintertodt herbs, lamps or extra potions are bonus progress.</div>
        <p>Complete any Easy tasks you have not already done. Bring a <strong>pickaxe, weapon, spade, 8,750 gp, tarromin potion (unf), 1 limpwurt root, fly fishing rod and feathers</strong>. If you do not already have a limpwurt root, kill <strong>Hill Giants</strong> until one drops.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Mine <strong>iron ore at the Mount Karuulm mine</strong>.</li>
          <li>Kill a <strong>Sand Crab</strong>.</li>
          <li>Hand in a <strong>book at the Arceuus Library</strong>.</li>
          <li>Steal from a <strong>Hosidius fruit stall</strong>.</li>
          <li>Browse the <strong>Warrens General Store</strong>.</li>
          <li>Take the boat to <strong>Land’s End</strong>.</li>
          <li>Pray at the altar on the <strong>top floor of Kourend Castle</strong>.</li>
          <li>Dig up <strong>saltpetre</strong> south-west of Hosidius.</li>
          <li>Speak to an <strong>Estate Agent</strong>, move your POH to <strong>Hosidius for 8,750 gp</strong>, then enter it through the Hosidius portal. Leave the house there for now.</li>
          <li>Complete a lap of either <strong>Shayzien Agility Course</strong>.</li>
          <li>In the <strong>Lovakengj pub</strong>, add the limpwurt root to the tarromin potion (unf) to make a <strong>strength potion</strong>.</li>
          <li>Fish a <strong>trout from the River Molch</strong> south-east of the Farming Guild.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Claim <strong>Rada’s blessing 1</strong> and the <strong>2,500 XP antique lamp</strong> from Elise at Kourend Castle. Put the lamp on <strong>Herblore</strong>.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Current-game note:</strong> Kourend Favour was removed in 2024. If an old guide tells you to gain Hosidius/Shayzien favour for these tasks, ignore it; the current Easy diary uses the tasks listed above.</div>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-myarm" number="6" title="Complete My Arm’s Big Adventure and let the quest finish 40 Herblore">
        <p>This is the payoff for the setup already completed: <strong>Eadgar’s Ruse</strong> is done, <strong>The Feud</strong> and <strong>Jungle Potion</strong> are complete, 1.3 reached at least <strong>60% Tai Bwo Wannai favour</strong>, and the Farming quest rewards already cover the <strong>29 Farming</strong> requirement.</p>
        <p>Bring <strong>3 buckets of Ugthanki dung</strong>, <strong>7 supercompost</strong>, climbing boots or Trollheim teleport access, some coins, and normal combat gear/food. A bucket can be obtained during the quest. The Trollheim tool leprechaun can supply a rake, seed dibber, spade and plant cure when needed.</p>
        <p>Complete <strong>My Arm’s Big Adventure</strong> from start to finish. During the Tai Bwo Wannai section, Murcaily gives you the separate <strong>hardy gout tuber</strong>; this is not the regular gout tuber banked during Cleanup in 1.3.</p>
        <p>The quest awards <strong>10,000 Herblore XP</strong> and <strong>5,000 Farming XP</strong>. Following the minimum XP route above, this is the point where <strong>40 Herblore is guaranteed</strong>. No extra herb-processing grind is required.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Favour and tuber are separate:</strong> My Arm spends 60 percentage points of Tai Bwo Wannai favour and supplies its own <em>hardy</em> tuber. A <em>regular</em> gout tuber, if you chose the optional Karamja Medium preparation, is not consumed here; bank it for that diary. Starting at the required 60% leaves 0%; starting at optional 100% leaves 40%.</div>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-smith" number="7" title="Reach 42 Smithing and finish the Chapter 1 jewelry stock">
        <p>Use Blast Furnace or a normal Smithing method to reach <strong>42 Smithing</strong>. Then bring your teleport jewelry up to the full Chapter 1 target:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>40 rings of dueling</strong></li>
          <li><strong>45 digsite pendants</strong></li>
          <li><strong>10 games necklaces</strong></li>
          <li><strong>8 bracelets of clay</strong></li>
          <li><strong>2 sapphire amulets</strong> — turn one into an <strong>amulet of magic</strong></li>
          <li><strong>1 ruby amulet</strong> — enchant it into an <strong>amulet of strength</strong></li>
          <li><strong>1 binding necklace</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Count what you made in 1.3 and only craft the difference. These quantities are intentionally explicit because the rest of Chapter 1 consumes teleport jewelry heavily.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-family" number="8" title="Complete Family Crest">
        <p>Complete <strong>Family Crest</strong> as a standalone quest. Keep the resulting gauntlet access; the quest is also an important prerequisite for later account progression.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-prayer" number="9" title="Bank about 75–80 dragon bones with a green → blue dragon hybrid, then reach 43 Prayer at the Chaos Altar">
        <p><strong>Dragonfire-protection check:</strong> before equipping the anti-dragon shield for this block, start <strong>Dragon Slayer I</strong> with the Guildmaster in the Champions' Guild (32 Quest Points are already secured), ask about protection, and collect the shield from Duke Horacio upstairs in Lumbridge Castle. Current mechanics require the quest to be <em>started</em> to equip the shield. This is a deliberate partial unlock for safe dragon gathering; finish the whole quest in 2.2.</p>
        <p><strong>The dragon bones are the Prayer method, but you do not need to spend the entire grind standing in the Wilderness.</strong> Green dragons are much faster and easier to kill, so start there while the area is quiet. If PK traffic becomes annoying—or if you simply do not want to risk another trip—finish the bone stack safely on <strong>Isle of Souls blue dragons</strong>. The bones are identical for Prayer purposes, so the two methods mix perfectly.</p>

        <p><strong>Target:</strong> aim for roughly <strong>75–80 dragon bones total</strong> before using the Chaos Altar. The exact number varies because earlier Prayer XP differs and the altar's 50% bone-save effect is random. If you already have Prayer XP above the route baseline, you may finish with fewer; stop immediately once you reach <strong>43 Prayer</strong>.</p>

        <h3>Part A — fast first batch: green dragons near Corporeal Beast</h3>
        <p><strong>Bring:</strong> a <strong>games necklace</strong>, <strong>water staff</strong>, <strong>air runes</strong>, <strong>chaos runes</strong>, <strong>mind runes</strong>, <strong>anti-dragon shield</strong>, <strong>amulet of magic</strong>, a <strong>looting bag</strong>, and cheap food such as the banked salmon. Wear only gear you are comfortable losing and leave your cash stack in the bank.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Use the games necklace to teleport to <strong>Corporeal Beast</strong>.</li>
          <li>Leave the cave into the Wilderness and run <strong>west</strong> to the nearby green dragons. Green dragons have a <strong>50% weakness to Water spells</strong>, which is why Water Bolt works so well here.</li>
          <li>Use <strong>Water Bolt</strong> while you have spare chaos runes. <strong>Preserve your final 100 chaos runes</strong> for later Chapter 2 needs; once you approach that reserve, switch to <strong>Water Strike</strong>.</li>
          <li>Pick up <strong>every dragon bone and every green dragonhide</strong>. Put hides in the looting bag first and keep bones in the inventory. Bank early rather than carrying a stack you would hate to lose.</li>
          <li><strong>Do not force the entire 75–80-bone grind here.</strong> Roughly <strong>30–40 green-dragon bones</strong> is a good fast first batch, but the real rule is simple: stay while it feels quiet and efficient; leave as soon as PK activity turns the method into a nuisance.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>Part B — safe finish: Isle of Souls blue dragons</h3>
        <p><strong>If Isle of Souls is new to you:</strong> this is a normal non-Wilderness island containing Soul Wars and a small dungeon. You do <strong>not</strong> need to play Soul Wars or complete its tutorial just to use the island. The easiest route is the <strong>Soul Wars minigame teleport</strong>, which lands you at Nomad's camp on the Isle of Souls. There is a bank in the Soul Wars lobby, making it a convenient place to resupply between dragon trips. <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Isle_of_Souls_Dungeon" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the OSRS Wiki Isle of Souls Dungeon page</a> if you want the map beside you on the first run.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Getting there:</strong> open the <strong>Grouping / Minigames</strong> interface and use the <strong>Soul Wars</strong> minigame teleport. If that is unavailable or on cooldown, fairy ring <strong>BJP</strong> lands on the northern coast of the Isle of Souls; the <strong>Soul Wars portal in Edgeville</strong> is another fallback. You do not need to enter a Soul Wars match.</li>
          <li>From the Soul Wars lobby, run <strong>north-east across the island</strong> to the dungeon entrance on the <strong>eastern side</strong> of the Isle of Souls and climb down.</li>
          <li><strong>Dungeon route:</strong> follow the dungeon around past the demon room and the fire-giant room. The <strong>third main room</strong> contains <strong>four adult blue dragons and three baby blue dragons</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>Safespot:</strong> the easiest landmark is the small <strong>cove containing two adamantite rocks</strong> on the eastern side of the blue-dragon room. Walk inside the stalagmites and cast from there; the adult dragons cannot reach you. Keep the <strong>anti-dragon shield equipped</strong> while moving through the dungeon.</li>
          <li><strong>Magic:</strong> blue dragons also have a <strong>50% Water weakness</strong>. Water Bolt is faster if you have spare chaos runes, but <strong>Water Strike is completely acceptable</strong> here and lets you preserve the 100-chaos-rune Chapter 2 reserve. With the safespot established, this is much lower-stress than the Wilderness method.</li>
          <li>Bank <strong>every dragon bone</strong> and, if you have inventory space, the <strong>blue dragonhides</strong> as well. A looting bag does not help outside the Wilderness, so simply bank at the Soul Wars lobby and run back. Continue until your combined green + blue dragon bones reach roughly <strong>75–80 total</strong>.</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="later-note"><strong>Keep one spare dragon bone banked after reaching 43 Prayer.</strong> Do not blindly empty the bank just because bones remain.</div>

        <h3>Part C — cash the bones in at the correct Wilderness Chaos Altar</h3>
        <div class="callout"><strong>There are two different Wilderness locations called the Chaos Temple. You want the small <em>Chaos Temple (hut)</em> in level 38 Wilderness, northwest of the Forgotten Cemetery and west of the Lava Maze.</strong> Do <strong>not</strong> use the level ~11 Chaos Temple near Ferox that is packed with Zombie Pirates and aggressive level-129 Elder Chaos Druids. Those druids can hit hard, repeatedly Tele Block you and pull you back toward them. If you see a crowd of druids/pirates, you are at the wrong temple. <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Wilderness" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the OSRS Wiki Wilderness map</a> and look for the level-38 <strong>Chaos Temple (hut)</strong>.</div>
        <p><strong>Default route:</strong> bank all combat gear at <strong>Ferox Enclave</strong>, take only <strong>one disposable inventory of dragon bones</strong>, then run north into deep Wilderness toward the <strong>Forgotten Cemetery</strong>. Continue <strong>northwest</strong> to the small level-38 temple just south of the Frozen Waste Plateau. If you already have a <strong>burning amulet</strong>, its <strong>Lava Maze</strong> teleport is a faster optional route; run west/southwest from the maze to the same level-38 hut.</p>
        <p>Inside the correct hut, use the bones on the <strong>Chaos Altar</strong> until the inventory is gone, then return and repeat. The altar gives the same <strong>350% XP</strong> as a gilded altar with both burners lit, and every offered bone has a <strong>50% chance not to be consumed</strong>, so a single bone can be offered multiple times. The hut itself is a known PK hotspot, which is why this guide deliberately risks only one bone inventory at a time.</p>

        <ExpandableNote label="Optional Wilderness tips" title="Give yourself the best chance to hop before a PKer lands the first hit">
          <p><strong>Before the first trip, set Private chat to Friends or Off.</strong> With Private set to Friends, players who are not on your Friends List see you as offline. That prevents a random PKer from simply adding your name and using private-chat status to follow your world between trips.</p>
          <p><strong>Keep the logout/world-switch interface ready while you offer bones.</strong> A very practical setup is to open the World Switcher and leave the cursor over a different world. If another player logs in beside the altar and has not hit you yet, switch immediately instead of waiting to decide whether they look dangerous. Once you are in combat, you cannot world-hop and must be out of combat for 10 seconds first.</p>
          <p><strong>Do not always hop to the next world in the list.</strong> Choosing a world several entries away is a reasonable anti-pattern habit because PKers often cycle through worlds looking for altar users. This is not protection or a guarantee—just a small way to make your next destination less predictable.</p>
          <p><strong>The first hit is the important one.</strong> There is very little you can do once a geared PKer has landed a freeze/Tele Block and put you in combat. Your best defence on this low-risk route is noticing the login quickly, hopping before the first attack, and never risking more than one inventory of bones.</p>
          <p><strong>Wine of Zamorak is for a clean suicide after the inventory is finished.</strong> When no player has damaged you, repeatedly taking the wine is a convenient way to die and return to your respawn point instead of running south. <strong>Do not rely on the wine to deny a PKer your remaining bones.</strong> If another player has already damaged you recently, the death uses Wilderness PvP death rules even if the wine deals the final point of damage; lost tradeable items are awarded under the PvP rules, generally to the player who dealt the most damage. If a PKer appears before touching you, hopping is the better escape attempt.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>

        <ExpandableNote label="Optional safety note" title="If you die and your bones are sitting in a dangerous gravestone">
          <p><strong>You usually do not need to keep death-running a dangerous PvM grave.</strong> A normal gravestone lasts for 15 minutes of logged-in time, and you can ask <strong>Death</strong> to collect its contents for you. Ferox Enclave has access to <strong>Death's Office</strong>, so if retrieving the grave itself is becoming a second death trap, bank anything you kept and go there instead.</p>
          <p><strong>The confusing “some items have been moved to Death's Office” message:</strong> if you die again while an existing grave already contains lots of normal unstackable items, the grave has a capacity rule. Excess items from the existing grave can be moved to Death's Office before the new death is added. That can make it look as though only the three items you kept on death survived, even though the rest may simply have been transferred to Death.</p>
          <p>For this route, ordinary dragon bones are individually worth less than 100,000 coins, so reclaiming those low-value items from Death is normally <strong>free</strong>. Valuable equipment can have reclamation fees, which is another reason the altar trips should contain essentially nothing except disposable bones.</p>
          <p><strong>Important:</strong> this explanation is for an ordinary gravestone/PvM death. A death involving another player in the Wilderness can use different item-loss rules. Always check the <strong>Items Kept on Death</strong> screen before risking anything you care about.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>

        <p><strong>Stop the moment you reach 43 Prayer.</strong> Any unused bones stay banked for later.</p>
        <div class="optional-efficiency"><strong>Why this hybrid is recommended:</strong> green dragons save time while the Wilderness is quiet; Isle of Souls removes the PK risk when it is not. The Chaos Altar still supplies the enormous Prayer-efficiency gain, but you only risk one small bone inventory at a time instead of your entire gathering grind.</div>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-imp" number="10" title="Complete Imp Catcher before finishing the Ranged target">
        <p>Check your bank for the four beads: <strong>red, yellow, black and white</strong>. If any are missing, kill imps around the <strong>Tower of Life / Ardougne area</strong> until you have one of each, then complete <strong>Imp Catcher</strong>.</p>
        <p>This is placed here because the Magic XP is still useful and it closes another Chapter 1 quest without creating a special trip later.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-slayer-ranged-30" number="11" title="Use one Slayer assignment to reach 18 Slayer, and train Ranged to 30 with the arrows you already made">
        <p><strong>Do not buy 1,200 throwing knives for the default route.</strong> The steel arrows made during the 46 Fletching block are now your deliberate early-Ranged ammunition. Shadow of the Storm already supplied <strong>10,000 Ranged XP</strong>, so the baseline account is around 27 Ranged and only needs a short push to 30.</p>
        <p>The Slayer gap is also intentionally small. By this point the guide has guaranteed <strong>3,000 Slayer XP</strong>: 1,000 from the Varrock Museum Natural History Quiz, 1,000 from A Porcine of Interest, and 1,000 from Recipe for Disaster: Freeing the Mountain Dwarf. That puts the baseline account at <strong>16 Slayer</strong>; level 18 is only <strong>523 XP</strong> farther away.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>If you already have an active Slayer task, use it.</strong> Otherwise go to <strong>Vannaka in Edgeville Dungeon</strong> and ask for an assignment. Vannaka requires 40 Combat, which the normal route has already cleared. Receiving his task also completes the corresponding <strong>Varrock Medium Diary</strong> task for later.</li>
          <li>Before leaving Vannaka, check whether the assignment needs a Slayer item such as earmuffs, a mirror shield or a bag of salt and buy it there if necessary.</li>
          <li>Use your <strong>best shortbow + steel arrows</strong> on the assignment whenever the monster/location is reasonable to range. A willow shortbow is already sufficient for the steel arrows; a maple shortbow at 30 is a small optional upgrade, not another required shopping stop.</li>
          <li><strong>Reach 18 Slayer.</strong> If the assignment is awkward to range, kill the monsters with melee/magic instead; the important requirement is 18 Slayer, not forcing every Slayer kill to double as Ranged training.</li>
          <li>If you are still below <strong>30 Ranged</strong> when 18 Slayer is reached, finish the short gap at <strong>Gemstone Crab</strong>, Sand Crabs or another comfortable safespot using the same steel arrows.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="callout"><strong>Why Vannaka instead of moving another quest forward?</strong> The only easy quest XP that belongs here has already been collected: the Museum quiz, A Porcine of Interest and the Mountain Dwarf subquest are the reason the account is already 16 Slayer. The next large quest reward, Wanted!, would require dragging Black Knights' Fortress and Recruitment Drive forward too. One small Slayer assignment is cleaner.</div>
        <p><a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Vannaka" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the OSRS Wiki Vannaka page</a> if you need the Edgeville Dungeon map or assignment details.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-animal-magnetism" number="12" title="Complete Ernest the Chicken, then Animal Magnetism for Ava’s attractor">
        <p>Now that you have <strong>18 Slayer and 30 Ranged</strong>, pull Animal Magnetism forward instead of waiting until Chapter 2. The other requirements are already covered: <strong>The Restless Ghost</strong> and <strong>Priest in Peril</strong> are complete, and the route is far above 19 Crafting / 35 Woodcutting.</p>
        <p><strong>First complete Ernest the Chicken</strong> in Draynor Manor. The quest does not require outside supplies; collect the pressure gauge, rubber tube and oil can inside/around the manor, solve the basement lever puzzle, and return to Professor Oddenstein. Quest Helper is fine for the lever puzzle.</p>
        <p><strong>Then prepare Animal Magnetism.</strong> Bring or obtain:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>1 mithril axe</strong> — the one deliberately bought at the Woodcutting Guild in 1.3 / the 60 Woodcutting checkpoint. No other axe substitutes for this quest step.</li>
          <li><strong>5 unnoted iron bars</strong>, <strong>hammer</strong> and your <strong>ghostspeak amulet</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>20 ecto-tokens.</strong> If you do not already have them, take 4 bones, 4 empty pots and 4 buckets to the Ectofuntus north of Port Phasmatys. Grind the four bones upstairs into bonemeal, fill the four buckets from the slime pool downstairs, worship four times, then talk to a ghost disciple while wearing the ghostspeak amulet. Each worship is worth 5 tokens.</li>
          <li><strong>1 hard leather.</strong> If missing, tan a cowhide into hard leather at Ellis in Al Kharid.</li>
          <li><strong>1 holy symbol.</strong> If missing, make an unblessed symbol from a silver bar + holy mould, string it with wool, then have Brother Jered upstairs in the Edgeville Monastery bless it. Your 43 Prayer already clears the monastery's 31 Prayer entry requirement.</li>
          <li><strong>Polished buttons.</strong> Check the bank first; if missing, pickpocket H.A.M. members in the hideout east of Draynor until you get buttons, then polish them by clicking them.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Complete <strong>Animal Magnetism</strong> from start to finish. At under 50 Ranged, Ava rewards <strong>Ava’s attractor</strong>. Wear it for the rest of the Ranged grind: it automatically recovers <strong>60% of fired ammunition</strong>, another 20% drops to the floor, and 20% breaks. Do not wear a metal torso that interferes with the device.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Reserve at least 75 steel arrows.</strong> At 50 Ranged, Ava can upgrade the attractor to <strong>Ava’s accumulator</strong> using 75 steel arrows; the accumulator automatically recovers 72% of fired ammunition.</div>
        <p><a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Animal_Magnetism" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the OSRS Wiki Animal Magnetism guide</a> if you want the dialogue/puzzle walkthrough while following this route's preparation list.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <TrainingOptions id="i14-ranged-training" skill="Ranged" target="45" why="The route has now converted the early arrow-making grind into useful combat progression: Shadow of the Storm gets you into the high 20s, the Vannaka/18-Slayer checkpoint gets you to 30, and Animal Magnetism gives Ava's attractor before the longer AFK portion. Finish 45 here so Temple of Ikov and the remaining quest combat are comfortable." recommended="Equip Ava's attractor, your best Ranged gear, a shortbow and the steel arrows already made during the Fletching block, then use Gemstone Crab in the Tlati Rainforest. Children of the Sun was completed in 1.2, so Varlamore is already unlocked. Travel to Varlamore, run south into the Tlati Rainforest, and enter any of the three Gemstone Crab mine caves; the caves route you to whichever mine currently has the crab. The crab effectively has infinite HP and stays in place for about 10 minutes; when it burrows, use the nearby cave to follow it. It gives 12.5% less XP per hit than normal targets, but the long idle windows and lack of overkill downtime make it excellent for this guide's AFK preference." relaxed="Sand Crabs or Ammonite Crabs are equally valid low-attention alternatives if you prefer familiar crab spots. With Ava's attractor equipped, most arrows return automatically; occasionally pick up the floor pile if you care about stretching the stack even farther." resource="Use the steel-arrow stock instead of buying a separate 1,200-knife stack. Keep at least 75 steel arrows banked for the Ava's accumulator upgrade at 50 Ranged. Loar Shades remain valid if you specifically want shade remains, but Chapter 1 does not require them." fast="Throwing knives remain the faster 2-tick alternative if you care about maximizing low-level Ranged XP/hour, and Ava's devices can recover knives too. They are no longer the default because the guide already produced thousands of arrows and prioritizes low-attention training." />

      <RouteStep id="i14-optional-melee" number="Optional" optional={true} title="Optional AFK break: take melee to 65 Attack / 65 Strength / 65 Defence at Gemstone Crab">
        <p>If you enjoy nearly second-screen combat, stay at <strong>Gemstone Crab</strong> after 45 Ranged and train melee to <strong>65 Attack, 65 Strength and 65 Defence</strong>.</p>
        <p>Equip the <strong>rune sword</strong> purchased in 1.3, your <strong>amulet of strength</strong>, and the best melee armour already in the bank. Attack the crab, leave auto-retaliate on, and move through the nearby cave when the crab relocates roughly every ten minutes. Train the three melee stats in whatever order you prefer.</p>
        <p><strong>Why 65/65/65?</strong> 65 Attack + 65 Strength is exactly the <strong>130 combined</strong> needed to enter the Warriors' Guild. 65 Defence already passes the <strong>60 Defence</strong> needed to equip a dragon defender and meets the <strong>actual, unboosted 65 Defence</strong> gate for King's Ransom. <strong>70 Defence</strong> for Piety still comes later.</p>
        <div class="optional-efficiency"><strong>This is genuinely optional.</strong> The mandatory Chapter 1 finish line remains about 46 Attack / 50 Strength / 34 Defence. If you skip this AFK detour, Chapter 2 will explicitly train the guild, defender and King's Ransom gates when they matter; it will never assume you took this branch.</div>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-journey" number="13" title="Complete Enlightened Journey">
        <p>Complete <strong>Enlightened Journey</strong> for balloon transport and its <strong>2,000 Crafting XP</strong>. You are already guaranteed <strong>30 Farming</strong> from the earlier quest XP math, so there is no extra Farming grind. Go directly to The Hand in the Sand next; A Soul’s Bane follows that quest.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-hand" number="14" title="Complete The Hand in the Sand immediately after Enlightened Journey">
        <p><strong>Read actual Crafting first:</strong> this quest needs <strong>actual 49 Crafting</strong> and 17 Thieving to start, with no prerequisite quest or mandatory fight. Thieving is already covered. If below 49, blow only enough of the protected 1,590-orb batch to reach 49; the orbs require actual 46, already checked in 1.3. You may use banked gems or separately sourced glass for a shortfall, but do not consume quest glass. At 49, blow <strong>one lantern lens</strong> from the glass reserved for this quest.</p>
        <p><strong>Bank check before Bert in Yanille:</strong> bring about <strong>150 coins</strong>, <strong>one empty vial</strong> (a second makes Zavistic's Port Sarim teleport convenient), <strong>one white berry</strong>, <strong>one lantern lens</strong>, <strong>five earth runes</strong>, and <strong>one empty bucket</strong> or a bucket of sand. If missing, buy a vial from a Herblore shop, a bucket from a general store and the earth runes from Betty in Port Sarim for 20 coins. Bring one beer and redberries if already banked; otherwise buy the beer at Yanille's Dragon Inn and redberries at Port Sarim's food store during the quest. The quest supplies its own sand from Sandy in Brimhaven; fill the bucket at Yanille's sandpit for the separate final bucket-of-sand step.</p>
        <p><strong>White-berry fallback now, before Farming Contracts:</strong> check the bank first. If none are banked, bring food and kill level-37 <strong>chaos druid warriors</strong> in the <strong>Yanille Agility Dungeon</strong> until one drops; the first balancing ledge needs 40 Agility, already covered. This is a repeatable one-item drop hunt (listed at 5/128 per kill), not a reason to force 59 Farming or visit Wilderness. Do not use the dungeon chaos altar: it drops you into poison spiders. A berry already grown/banked is fine.</p>
        <p>Start with <strong>Bert by the Yanille sandpit</strong>, then ring the bell <strong>outside</strong> the Wizards' Guild to call Zavistic Rarve. You do <strong>not</strong> need the normal guild-entry Magic level for this quest. Follow Bert's/Sandy's rota and Betty's truth-serum sequence, visit Entrana briefly, and finish the quest for <strong>9,000 Crafting XP</strong>, 1,000 Thieving XP and Bert's daily sand option. The <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/The_Hand_in_the_Sand/Quick_guide" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">current quick guide</a> covers dialogue if needed.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-glass" number="15" title="Complete A Soul’s Bane and finish the 1,590 unpowered orbs">
        <p>Complete <strong>A Soul’s Bane</strong> now; it was deliberately placed after Enlightened Journey → The Hand in the Sand. This is the deadline for the orb stock, <em>not</em> the time to decide on permanent 58 Crafting.</p>
        <p>Part 1.3 already established the full glass plan, and this is the deadline. Your original <strong>1,600 molten glass</strong> should be accounted for as:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>1,590 unpowered orbs</strong></li>
          <li><strong>6 molten glass</strong> reserved for <strong>Desert Treasure</strong></li>
          <li><strong>1 molten glass</strong> already blown into the <strong>lantern lens</strong> for <strong>The Hand in the Sand</strong></li>
          <li><strong>1 molten glass used for the birdhouse crafting table 2</strong> in 1.3, plus <strong>up to 2 spare molten glass</strong>; one may have become Pirate Pete's fishbowl, leaving one for Chapter 2</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If you split the bankstanding grind across the chapter, <strong>finish every remaining unpowered orb now</strong>. The 1,590 orbs give <strong>83,475 Crafting XP</strong>. At an actual-46 floor established <em>after</em> smelting, the orbs alone end at <strong>151,458 XP</strong>, not 183,458: the earlier 32,000 smelting XP is already inside that level-46 floor. Subsequent guaranteed quest XP, the lens and any jewellery raise the live total. <strong>Do not train to 58 here:</strong> the final handoff checks the actual level after the remaining quests.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-quests" number="16" title="Complete Biohazard and Ardougne Easy, then Watchtower">
        <p>Complete Biohazard, the short diary, then Watchtower; rebank between activities:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Biohazard</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Ardougne Easy Diary</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Watchtower</strong></li>
        </ol>
<p><strong>After Biohazard, finish Ardougne Easy before leaving the area.</strong> Rune Mysteries and the 5-Thieving gate are already covered. Open the <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Ardougne_Diary#Easy" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Easy task list</a> and check each entry: Cromperty's essence teleport, East Ardougne cake and silk stalls (sell a silk for 60 coins), the church altar, one <em>started</em> Fishing Trawler trip (the outfit is not required for this task), Biohazard's Combat Training Camp, Tindel Marchant's rusty-sword identification, the Ardougne Wilderness lever, Aleck's Yanille shop and Probita's pet check. Pickpocket H.A.M. members for a rusty sword if none is banked; Tindel charges 100 coins and a rare junk result requires another sword. For the lever, <strong>bank valuables first</strong>, pull it into Wilderness, then return immediately. Claim <strong>Ardougne cloak 1</strong>, its unlimited monastery teleport and the 2,500-XP lamp (use it on Herblore; later conservative minimums do not depend on it); its other useful payoff is <strong>200 instead of 100 death runes</strong> when a grown cat is traded to a West Ardougne civilian. This diary has a named rune/travel use, not a generic efficiency checkbox.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Optional background cat loop:</strong> after the diary, let a kitten follow during suitable long sessions, feed/stroke it as needed (stackable raw karambwanji become convenient after Tai Bwo Wannai Trio), then trade an <em>extra</em> grown cat in West Ardougne for 200 death runes. Growth takes roughly three hours <em>following</em>, not three hours in the bank. Do not promise a fixed number of cats/runes. <strong>Reserve one grown, non-overgrown cat for RFD Evil Dave in 2.4</strong> before trading extras; the follower slot can be left free whenever an activity needs it.</div>
        <p>My Arm’s Big Adventure has deliberately been moved earlier in this page so the <strong>40 Herblore breakpoint is resolved immediately after Eadgar’s Ruse and the Kourend Easy lamp</strong>, rather than being hidden inside an unrelated multi-quest block.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <TrainingOptions id="i14-farming-training" informational={true} skill="Farming" target="start the passive tree-run lane" why="Actual 62 opens boosted medium Farming Contracts and 65 removes the boost, but neither is the next quest's hard gate. Keep doing the guide while trees grow; check actual 49 at Fairytale II and actual 70 before Song of the Elves." recommended="At a convenient login, check mature ordinary and fruit trees, collect their health-check XP, replant from banked seeds, then go play. The run takes attention; the growing happens passively even while logged out." relaxed="Do only a few accessible patches, or fruit trees when ordinary seeds are scarce. Return at a later session; no alarm, second daily circuit or nine-patch quota." resource="Birdhouses may yield seed nests; easy contracts give random seed packs once available. Herbs support potions, occasional hardwood checks give XP, and giant seaweed mainly supports later Crafting." fast="Tithe Farm is an optional timed, higher-input bridge for a chosen level or reward. You never need to complete its shop to progress." />

      <p><strong>Contract milestones, not a Chapter 1 deadline:</strong> the Farming Guild's beginner wing and Jane's easy contracts need <strong>45 Farming</strong>; a Garden pie can reach that from <strong>actual 42</strong>. At <strong>actual 62</strong>, the same +3 boost reaches the <strong>65</strong> intermediate wing and medium contracts. At <strong>actual 65</strong>, mediums need no pie. Reach the Guild via fairy ring <strong>CIR</strong> with your dramen staff, then walk south-west. You must be boosted when asking Jane for <em>each</em> higher-tier contract, including “another contract”; existing easy contracts cannot be cancelled, whereas medium/hard contracts can be downgraded. Start with attainable easy assignments using banked potato, onion, cabbage or flower seeds rather than waiting for a high-level preplant layout. A completed assignment gives a <strong>random seed pack</strong>, never a chosen tree seed. Once the intermediate wing is available, add its ordinary tree patch to Guild visits; its fruit-tree patch is in the <strong>85-Farming</strong> wing, not this early circuit. <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Farming_contract" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Contract rules</a>.</p>

      <div class="callout"><strong>Garden pie supply for passive players too:</strong> a Garden pie gives <strong>+3 Farming</strong>. The <strong>Cooks’ Guild west of Varrock</strong> requires 32 Cooking and a chef's hat; this route already exceeds the Cooking level. Use a banked hat or buy one from <strong>Shayzien Styles</strong>. Romily Weaklax's Pie Shop inside has a base stock of <strong>two Garden pies at 24 coins each</strong> and restocks; hop or return later only if you actually need more. Refresh the boost before each boosted Guild entry/contract request. If you choose Tithe at actual 51–53, a pie can also unlock Bologano seeds at effective 54 while taking and planting them.</div>

      <ExpandableNote label="First-time tree route" title="Your daily tree route: collect XP, replant, then keep playing">
        <p><strong>Prepare saplings at the bank:</strong> choose unlocked seeds that are actually available, keeping the reserved Fossil Island teak seeds and quest-specific seeds aside. Put each ordinary or fruit-tree seed into a <strong>filled plant pot</strong> using a <strong>gardening trowel</strong>, water the seedling once with a watering can, then wait a few minutes for a plantable sapling. Make <em>up to</em> five ordinary-tree and four fruit-tree saplings; different species and a smaller run are fine. Bank unused seeds, pots and tools. Magic tree seeds require <strong>75 Farming</strong> and are <strong>not</strong> part of this 62–65 plan: retain them for later high-level Farming, contracts or optional diaries. No mandatory quest in this route consumes a magic seed; the separate <strong>16 magic logs</strong> allocation is sourced independently.</p>
        <p><strong>Take on the run:</strong> available saplings, spade, rake (unless Auto-weed is already active and the patch is clear), ordinary teleport runes or tablets, coins for travel and tree removal, and any protection payments or compost. Weight-reducing clothes help but are optional. No Graceful, seed box, bottomless bucket, Farmer's outfit, Humidify, maxed POH or timer plugin is assumed. Ordinary Lumbridge/Falador/Varrock/Camelot teleport <em>spells</em> need the standard spellbook; on Ancients or Lunar, use already-made city tablets where stocked, teleport jewellery/other unlocked transport plus short walks, or switch back at the proper altar before casting. Do not assume an unlimited tablet bank.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Lumbridge — ordinary tree:</strong> Lumbridge teleport, then the patch west of/behind Lumbridge Castle.</li>
          <li><strong>Falador — ordinary tree:</strong> ordinary Falador teleport, then walk to Falador Park. A charged Ring of wealth is not required.</li>
          <li><strong>Taverley — ordinary tree:</strong> continue north-west from Falador to the Taverley patch; no redirected tablet or house move.</li>
          <li><strong>Varrock — ordinary tree:</strong> Varrock teleport, then the patch in the palace courtyard.</li>
          <li><strong>Tree Gnome Stronghold — ordinary tree <em>and</em> fruit tree:</strong> from Varrock go to the Grand Exchange spirit tree and ride to the Stronghold. Visit the ordinary patch south-west of the Stronghold spirit tree, then the fruit patch just east of that spirit tree. The Grand Tree and Tree Gnome Village already unlocked this network.</li>
          <li><strong>Tree Gnome Village — fruit tree:</strong> take the spirit tree to the Village maze, ask <strong>Elkoy</strong> to escort you out, then head west/south-west to the fruit patch outside the maze.</li>
          <li><strong>Catherby — fruit tree:</strong> Camelot teleport, run to the fruit patch east of Catherby near the mountain/beach, and use Catherby bank if you need a resupply.</li>
          <li><strong>Brimhaven — fruit tree:</strong> from Catherby docks charter to Brimhaven, then walk north to its patch. The listed fare is <strong>480 coins</strong> before discounts; the already-unlocked <strong>ring of charos(a)</strong> halves it to 240 while worn. Cabin Fever, a Brimhaven house and a planted spirit tree are not assumed.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>That is <strong>eight stops and nine patches</strong>. On the first outing you only plant; there is no mature-tree health-check jackpot yet. Later, at each mature tree: <strong>check health</strong> for the main Farming XP, pick useful fruit (including possible protection fruit), pay the gardener <strong>200 coins</strong> to remove the old tree, plant a replacement sapling, then pay for protection <em>or</em> compost the patch and leave. The 200-coin clearing fee is separate from the new tree's protection payment. Tree saplings do not need repeated watering once planted outdoors. A grown tree can instead be chopped for Woodcutting and its stump dug for optional roots; neither is repeatable large Farming XP from leaving the same tree there. Paying for clearing keeps the usual run short.</p>
        <div class="crop-table-wrap"><table class="crop-table"><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Tree / seed</th><th>Growth</th><th>Health-check XP</th><th>Gardener protection</th></tr></thead><tbody>
          <tr><td>15</td><td>Oak / acorn</td><td>3h 20m</td><td>467.3</td><td>1 basket of tomatoes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30</td><td>Willow</td><td>~4h</td><td>1,456.5</td><td>1 basket of apples</td></tr>
          <tr><td>45</td><td>Maple</td><td>5h 20m</td><td>3,403.4</td><td>1 basket of oranges</td></tr>
          <tr><td>60</td><td>Yew</td><td>6h 40m</td><td>7,069.9</td><td>10 cactus spines</td></tr>
          <tr><td>27</td><td>Apple (fruit)</td><td>16h</td><td>1,199.5</td><td>9 sweetcorn</td></tr>
          <tr><td>33</td><td>Banana (fruit)</td><td>16h</td><td>1,750.5</td><td>4 baskets of apples</td></tr>
          <tr><td>39</td><td>Orange (fruit)</td><td>16h</td><td>2,470.2</td><td>3 baskets of strawberries</td></tr>
          <tr><td>42</td><td>Curry (fruit)</td><td>16h</td><td>2,906.9</td><td>5 baskets of bananas</td></tr>
          <tr><td>51</td><td>Pineapple (fruit)</td><td>16h</td><td>4,605.7</td><td>10 watermelons</td></tr>
          <tr><td>57</td><td>Papaya (fruit)</td><td>16h</td><td>6,146.4</td><td>10 pineapples</td></tr>
        </tbody></table></div>
        <p>These are <strong>health-check</strong> XP figures, excluding planting and fruit picking; growth times are approximate. Use the highest useful unlocked seeds you actually have, not an imaginary daily supply. For scale only, five successful maple checks plus four successful pineapple checks yield <strong>5 × 3,403.4 + 4 × 4,605.7 ≈ 35,440 Farming XP</strong> before planting/fruit XP. That assumes all nine seeds, relevant levels and healthy mature trees; it is <em>not</em> a guaranteed Chapter 1 daily reward. One circuit at a convenient login is enough; ordinary trees can be revisited sooner if you want, but there is no required second daily run. <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Farming_training#Tree_runs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Tree-run reference</a>.</p>
        <p><strong>Protection without a shopping marathon:</strong> ordinary apples, bananas, tomatoes and pineapples are sold by <strong>Cobado's Groceries in Civitas illa Fortis</strong>; <strong>Funch's Fine Groceries in the Grand Tree</strong> sells oranges and pineapples. Buy empty baskets at Farming shops and fill each basket with <strong>five</strong> of the named fruit where the table asks for a basket; gardeners accept noted payments where supported. Sweetcorn and watermelons come from your own allotments or existing bank, not a guaranteed shop stock. If strawberry baskets, ten watermelons, cactus spines or another payment are awkward, use compost instead and accept disease risk. For ultracompost, use <strong>two volcanic ash on one bucket of supercompost</strong> (Fossil Island ash is mineable); it greatly reduces but does not eliminate disease. It does not multiply tree fruit/health-check XP. Protect valuable scarce seeds when the payment is affordable; do not spend twenty minutes shopping for every cheap tree.</p>
        <p><strong>Fossil Island hardwood exception:</strong> check the health of the two <strong>western teak trees</strong> from the earlier Chapter 2 timber prep, but <strong>leave them planted</strong> while they are supplying Construction logs. Do not clear and replant them just for another Farming check. The third hardwood patch can rotate with a banked suitable seed on an occasional multi-day visit, not this daily circuit. After 2.1 has enough timber, rotating the resident teaks is optional.</p>
      </ExpandableNote>

      <ExpandableNote label="Seed supply" title="Running low on tree seeds?">
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Search the bank first:</strong> use existing lower-tier tree/fruit saplings and seeds; search unsearched seed nests. Collect already-earned unclaimed Wintertodt rewards if any remain, but do not return for another long Wintertodt grind.</li>
          <li><strong>Birdhouses are available now:</strong> use the <a href={`${base}intermediate/chapter-1-3/#i13-birdhouses`}>Chapter 1.3 birdhouse primer</a>. Bring a Digsite pendant, hammer/chisel, suitable logs and cheap bait seeds; collect houses, reuse clockworks, reset them, then search seed nests. Do it when convenient, not on a 50-minute alarm. Maple/pineapple seeds are possible but random, never promised.</li>
          <li><strong>Try attainable Farming Contracts:</strong> at actual 45 (or 42 with a Garden pie), fairy ring CIR plus a south-west walk reaches Jane and easy contracts. Bring a few basic allotment/flower/bush seeds from the bank or seed shop, accept a crop you can actually grow, harvest/check it inside the Guild, then open the <em>random</em> seed pack. Easy contracts cannot be cancelled; medium/hard can be downgraded. Boost again before requesting another boosted tier. This is a seed source, not guaranteed tree-seed self-sufficiency.</li>
          <li><strong>Later, not now:</strong> Managing Miscellania unlocks in <strong>Chapter 2.3</strong>. Maple workers can add nests/seeds then, but consume treasury coins; do not bring the kingdom quest forward or drain herb/timber funding just to claim seed income.</li>
          <li><strong>If tree seeds run dry:</strong> run fewer ordinary patches, keep available fruit/hardwoods going, and grow useful herbs or allotments while waiting. <strong>Olivia in Draynor</strong> stocks basic allotment/hops/flower seeds; Master Farmers can supplement herbs/allotments, but neither reliably supplies the tree seeds for this circuit. No Grand Exchange, compulsory Mole, Puro-Puro, bossing or Tithe is required. A dry bank may slow passive Farming; check your live level at the later 49/70 consumers and use a bounded active bridge only if you choose or need it.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Keep a <em>modest</em> useful contract buffer if one exists, but do not freeze your last five ordinary seeds for hypothetical future assignments. Preserve named teak/quest seeds. Banked <strong>magic seeds</strong> remain untouched here until 75 Farming; they are not substitutes for the <strong>16 magic logs</strong> reserved for quests.</p>
      </ExpandableNote>

      <p><strong>Small side crops are optional:</strong> on tree-route days, plant useful potion herbs in convenient unlocked patches such as Catherby, Ardougne (using the cloak/monastery route) or the Hosidius patch; use the Tool Leprechaun's produce-noting service when a harvest fills your inventory. Whiteberry bushes and limpwurt roots are useful when stocked, but Chapter 1's single required whiteberry already has its explicit drop fallback. Birdhouses supply Hunter XP and possible seed nests; giant seaweed at the two underwater Fossil Island patches is mainly a future <strong>Crafting</strong> supply, not a Farming XP obligation. Visit these only when they fit your session.</p>

      <ExpandableNote label="Optional active method" title="Optional active method: Tithe Farm and targeted rewards">
        <p><strong>Choose this only for a specific XP bridge or reward.</strong> Tithe Farm is in south-east Hosidius; from the already-moved Hosidius POH, leave the house and head east. Bring a spade, seed dibber and about 8–10 filled watering cans; the nearby Tool Leprechaun can supply ordinary tools. Entry begins at <strong>34 Farming</strong>, covered by this chapter's quests. It is a timed planting/watering activity, not AFK, and needs no Humidify.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Take the highest seeds your <em>current effective level</em> allows: Golovanova at 34, Bologano at 54 or Logavano at 74. Boosts must be live while taking/planting seeds, not throughout harvest.</li>
          <li>Plant and immediately water <strong>8–20 patches</strong>; return for <strong>two more watering passes</strong> as they advance, then harvest and deposit the fruit. A comfortable <strong>20-plant cycle is fully valid</strong>. There is no obligation to graduate to 24–25 plants or use animation skips. Mature plants no longer decay, but missing an earlier timed watering can kill them.</li>
          <li>A full <strong>100-fruit</strong> sack earns <strong>35 points</strong> and includes the large 75th-fruit XP bonus. That can be <strong>five successful 20-plant cycles</strong>, not 100 simultaneous plants. Break at a natural point and stop when the chosen XP gap or reward is met.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Spend only for a benefit you want:</strong> check existing points first. <strong>Auto-weed (50)</strong> is the smallest useful crop-run convenience; from zero points two complete 100-fruit batches cover it. Turn it on at Gricoller's reward shop, rake any weeds already present once, then ordinary patches stay clear while enabled. Plain raking works perfectly without it. <strong>Herb sack (250)</strong> is useful later for grimy-herb drops and requires <strong>actual 58 Herblore to buy/use</strong>; alternatively it costs <strong>750 Slayer reward points</strong>, which are scarce and need not be spent here. <strong>Seed box (250)</strong> holds up to six <em>seed types</em>, not saplings, and is only carrying convenience; it is not automatically more valuable than the Herb sack for someone who dislikes Tithe. From zero, either 250-point item takes about <strong>eight complete 100-fruit batches</strong>, a very different commitment from Auto-weed. Gricoller's can (200) and the Farmer's outfit (400 total) are optional investments. No reward is a required Chapter 1 or later graduation item.</p>
        <p>After any Tithe session, resume ordinary quests and the passive crop routine. If giant seaweed interests you, its two underwater Fossil Island patches require 23 Farming and seaweed spores; dive from the northern rowboat with Farming tools and compost, mind oxygen without diving gear, and collect occasional seafloor spore spawns. Growth is roughly 40 minutes and its payoff is later Crafting supplies. Neither seaweed nor a Tithe shop purchase is required progress. <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Tithe_Farm" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Tithe Farm mechanics and rewards</a>.</p>
      </ExpandableNote>

      <TrainingOptions id="i14-hunter-training" skill="Hunter" target="46" why="Birdhouses should already have supplied much of this level. 46 is the Chapter 1 benchmark and leaves the account in a good place for future Hunter content." recommended="Keep doing birdhouse runs until 46 Hunter. If you are only a few levels short, there is no need to force a separate grind." relaxed="Wait for additional birdhouse cycles across normal play sessions." resource="If you want to finish immediately, use a conventional Hunter method appropriate to your level." fast="Kebbit/chinchompa routes are available if you want faster active XP." />

      <RouteStep id="i14-ikov" number="17" title="Complete Temple of Ikov without hunting a yew longbow">
        <p>Bring <strong>20 unnoted limpwurt roots</strong>, a light source, weight-reducing gear, and <strong>darts or throwing knives</strong>. Complete <strong>Temple of Ikov</strong>.</p>
        <p>You do <strong>not</strong> need to grind Puro-Puro for a yew longbow. In the ice chamber, search the chests until you obtain at least <strong>1 ice arrow</strong>. Equip the ice arrow in your ammo slot, then use darts/throwing knives on the Fire Warrior of Lesarkus; the current quest mechanics allow thrown weapons to bypass the normal yew-bow requirement.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-dt" number="18" title="Complete Desert Treasure from start to finish">
        <p>With <strong>The Dig Site, The Tourist Trap, Troll Stronghold, Priest in Peril, Waterfall Quest</strong> and <strong>Temple of Ikov</strong> completed, all quest prerequisites are done. <strong>Check actual 53 Thieving now:</strong> the 1.2 cash stop guarantees only actual 50, not 53. Count the intervening mandatory quest XP; if still short, use Wealthy Citizens/Stealing Valuables or Ardougne Knights for only the live remainder, then stop at 53. This is needed to pick the Bandit Camp chest's locks and is not boostable. The other hard gates—<strong>50 Magic, 50 Firemaking and 10 Slayer</strong>—are covered by the route.</p>
        <p><strong>Before starting, withdraw or confirm you have:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>12 magic logs</strong>, <strong>6 steel bars</strong> and the <strong>6 molten glass</strong> reserved earlier</li>
          <li><strong>ashes, charcoal, 1 blood rune, bones and 1 silver bar</strong></li>
          <li><strong>garlic + pestle and mortar</strong> for garlic powder, <strong>spice</strong>, and one accepted sweet such as a chocolate bar/cake</li>
          <li><strong>climbing boots + 1 iron bar</strong> so Dunstan can make the spiked boots</li>
          <li>the <strong>gas mask</strong> kept from Plague City, a <strong>tinderbox</strong>, and roughly <strong>30-50 lockpicks</strong></li>
          <li>good food, several prayer/restoration potions, energy potions and antipoison</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Complete <strong>Desert Treasure</strong> from start to finish. The gas mask is the important easy-to-miss item: keep it equipped in the Smoke Dungeon.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>One worthwhile future pickup:</strong> if your Kamil/ice route already gives you easy access to <strong>1 Trollweiss</strong>, bank it for Troll Romance. If getting it would require starting another quest or assembling a separate sled setup, skip it and collect it during Troll Romance later.</div>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-paddewwa" number="19" title="Make all 108 Paddewwa teleport tablets now">
        <p>After Desert Treasure, switch to Ancient Magicks and prepare:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>108 soft clay</strong></li>
          <li><strong>108 air runes</strong></li>
          <li><strong>216 law runes</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Fire staff</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Make <strong>108 Paddewwa teleport tablets</strong>. The Earth-orb grind itself uses only part of this stack, but making the full batch now avoids repeating the spellbook/lectern setup later.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <TrainingOptions id="i14-construction-training" skill="Construction" target="39" why="Scrambled! has a hard 38 Construction requirement, while the Chapter 1 / early Chapter 2 handoff is cleaner at 39. Getting Ahead and Wintertodt repairs help, but neither guarantees the level, so finish it explicitly here." recommended="Use Mahogany Homes with your banked oak planks until 39. This is the preferred lower-intensity route." relaxed="Break the contracts into short sessions around birdhouse runs or Farming timers." resource="Use existing planks first and buy only what you need to close the gap." fast="Oak larders/furniture in your POH are faster if you want the level immediately." />

      <RouteStep id="i14-varlamore" number="20" title="Finish the Varlamore unlocks and buy the cosmic-rune total">
        <p><strong>Children of the Sun was completed in 1.2</strong>, so the Varlamore chain is ready. Complete <strong>Twilight’s Promise</strong>, then—with <strong>39 Construction</strong> now guaranteed—complete <strong>Scrambled!</strong>. Then use the Tal Teklan rune shop and other existing stock to bring your bank to <strong>4,900 cosmic runes total</strong>.</p>
        <p>The orb batch consumes <strong>4,770 cosmic runes</strong>—three per orb—so 4,900 leaves a small buffer for enchanting and mistakes. The 1.2 coin target is an opening reserve, <em>not</em> a guaranteed balance after MTA, sand, Magic training and quest purchases. Check liquid coins before buying: if short, do a bounded Wealthy Citizen/house-valuables refill in Civitas illa Fortis and return for the remaining runes; do not count banked alchables as spendable coins.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-orbs" number="21" title="Charge all 1,590 Earth orbs with the simple route">
        <p><strong>Magic and spellbook checkpoint:</strong> read your actual Magic XP after MTA, quests and jewellery. Charge Earth Orb needs <strong>60 Magic on the standard spellbook</strong>, and early 2.1 needs <strong>actual 63 Magic</strong> to boost into the Wizards' Guild. If below 63, train only the live remainder with ordinary elemental combat on safe, low-defence targets or sensible banked High Alchemy, buying runes in small batches; do not spend the nature runes intended for the 2.1 battlestaves without budgeting replacements. <strong>Switch back from Ancient Magicks to the standard spellbook at the Desert Pyramid before the first orb trip.</strong> Paddewwa tablets remain usable as teleports after switching.</p>
        <p>Use the low-complexity version rather than the gravestone/corpse method:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Equip an <strong>earth staff</strong> and a bank teleport such as a ring of dueling.</li>
          <li>Withdraw <strong>25 unpowered orbs</strong>, <strong>75 cosmic runes</strong> and <strong>1 Paddewwa tablet</strong>.</li>
          <li>Break the Paddewwa tablet and run through Edgeville Dungeon to the <strong>Earth Obelisk</strong>.</li>
          <li>Cast Charge Earth Orb until all 25 are charged.</li>
          <li>Teleport to a bank, deposit the Earth orbs, restock, repeat.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Continue until all <strong>1,590</strong> are charged. This is slower than an optimized death/gravestone storage route, but it is much easier to follow and produces the same Chapter 2 resource.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i14-crafting-handoff" number="22" title="After quest XP, secure the functional Crafting-58 battlestaff start">
        <p><strong>Quest XP first → live XP check second → material grind or temporary boost last.</strong> Animal Magnetism (+1,000 Crafting), Enlightened Journey (+2,000), and The Hand in the Sand (+9,000) are now complete; finish/check the 1,590 unpowered orbs and the full jewellery stock before deciding. Read actual Crafting XP, not an old projected level. Preserve the 1,590 charged Earth orbs and every named quest resource.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Already actual 58+:</strong> you can attach Earth orbs to battlestaves in 2.1 without any pie. Do not hunt Sulliuscep caps.</li>
          <li><strong>Below 58, with caps/pies:</strong> mushroom pie gives +4 Crafting per bite. If five caps arrived during Woodcutting, make four pies (and keep one cap spare): mix four flour with water, put four pastry dough in four pie dishes, add one cap to each and bake at a range with your 60 Cooking. Bank the pies for 2.1. If you have fewer, use only a stock you judge sufficient to bridge the live XP gap; otherwise use the permanent-level option.</li>
          <li><strong>Below 58, without enough pies:</strong> train <em>only the live XP remainder</em> to actual 58 with banked gems or separately gathered sand/soda ash/glass. Do not take from the protected orb or quest supplies. Once the skill tab reads 58, stop.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>This checkbox is only the Crafting-readiness outcome:</strong> actual 58 Crafting or sufficient mushroom-pie boosts to begin Earth battlestaves. Earlier steps already track actual 70 Woodcutting and 1,590 charged Earth orbs; do not count either again here. Caps and extra-glass training are methods, not separate required progress.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <section class="completion-panel">
        <div class="section-label">Chapter 2 handoff check</div>
        <h3>Do not leave Chapter 1 with an invisible dependency missing.</h3>
        <p>Before moving into the Intermediate Chapter 2 route, confirm the following bank/state. These are the Chapter 1 resources that become relevant early enough in Chapter 2 that collecting them here is worthwhile:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>1,590 charged Earth orbs</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Actual 58 Crafting or sufficient banked mushroom-pie boosts</strong> for the 2.1 Earth-battlestaff start; if naturally 58+, no pies or caps are required</li>
          <li><strong>2,000 stardust</strong>, unspent, for the Celestial ring</li>
          <li><strong>At least 100 chaos runes</strong> still banked; the green-dragon step deliberately swaps to Water Strike instead of consuming this reserve</li>
          <li><strong>At least 1 spare molten glass</strong>; the 1,600-glass batch leaves two initially after 1,590 orbs + 6 Desert Treasure glass + 1 Hand in the Sand lens + 1 crafting-table glass. If Pirate Pete used one for a fishbowl, keep the other for early Chapter 2</li>
          <li><strong>Only if obtained:</strong> a regular gout tuber for a future optional Karamja Medium Diary; it has no Chapter 2 mandatory consumer</li>
          <li><strong>1 harralander</strong> reserved for Heroes' Quest</li>
          <li><strong>1 bucket of sap</strong> reserved for Icthlarin's Little Helper</li>
          <li><strong>10 buckets of slime</strong> from 1.2; early Chapter 2 uses most of them around the Ectofuntus/Ghosts Ahoy sequence</li>
          <li><strong>15 salmon</strong> reserved from the Fishing section for the later Myreque quest chain</li>
          <li><strong>Heavy fishing rod</strong> kept from Barbarian Training; Chapter 2 returns to Barbarian Fishing</li>
          <li><strong>2 teak trees planted/growing</strong> in the western Fossil Island hardwood patches if seed RNG allowed it</li>
          <li><strong>Ava’s attractor</strong> unlocked from Animal Magnetism and <strong>at least 75 steel arrows</strong> kept available for the accumulator upgrade at 50 Ranged</li>
          <li><strong>Kharedst's memoirs</strong>, <strong>crystal saw</strong>, <strong>gas mask</strong>, <strong>rune sword</strong>, green d'hide chaps/vambraces and your teleport jewelry</li>
          <li><strong>2 spare goutweed</strong> banked for much later quest use</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Dragon Slayer I was started only to obtain and equip the anti-dragon shield</strong> for the Prayer block; it is finished coherently in 2.2. Monkey Madness I, Black Knights' Fortress, Death to the Dorgeshuun, Underground Pass and Alfred Grimhand's Barcrawl do not need to be partially started.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="chapter-complete" class="completion-panel">
        <div class="section-label">Chapter 1 complete</div>
        <h3>The account now has a midgame backbone.</h3>
        <p>You now have passive Hunter, fairy rings, a <strong>Farming tree/fruit-tree routine</strong> with easy contracts when eligible, protection prayers, Ava’s attractor, Ancient Magicks, a practical teleport network, the exact early-Chapter-2 carryovers above, and <strong>1,590 Earth orbs</strong> ready for the battlestaff/alchemy money loop. Farming may still be below 62; keep checking live levels at the later quest gates.</p>
      </section>

